{"name":"Mother Nature","hex":"#bde1c4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Mother Nature","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A personification of nature as woman, emphasizing fertility and fecundity."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Mother_Nature"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mother Nature","blocks":[{"text":"Mother Nature is a personification of nature that focuses on the life-giving and nurturing aspects of nature by embodying it, in the form of a mother or mother goddess."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Joseph_Werner_-_Diana_of_Ephesus_as_allegory_of_Nature_-_ca._1680.jpg/330px-Joseph_Werner_-_Diana_of_Ephesus_as_allegory_of_Nature_-_ca._1680.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mother_Nature","wikidata":"Q1402540"},{"title":"Mother Nature (Eva Marija song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Mother Nature\" is a song by Luxembourgish singer-songwriter and violinist Eva Marija. It represented Luxembourg at the Eurovision Song Contest 2026. It is the first Luxembourgish entry in the contest to be performed entirely in English, and the first of Luxembourg's entries to fail to qualify since their return to the contest in 2024."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/5e/Eva_Marija_-_Mother_Nature.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mother_Nature_(Eva_Marija_song)","wikidata":"Q137883024"},{"title":"Mother","blocks":[{"text":"A mother, mom, mum, mommy, or mummy is the female parent of a child. A woman may be considered a mother by virtue of having given birth, by raising a child who may or may not be her biological offspring, or by supplying her ovum for fertilisation in the case of gestational surrogacy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Portrait_of_a_woman_holding_a_baby_%28I0024828%29.jpg/330px-Portrait_of_a_woman_holding_a_baby_%28I0024828%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mother","wikidata":"Q7560"},{"title":"Nature","blocks":[{"text":"Nature, in the broadest sense, is the physical world or universe. In this general sense nature refers to the laws, elements and phenomena of the physical world, including life. Although humans are part of nature, human activity or humans as a whole are often described as at times at odds, or outright separate and even superior to nature."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Startrails_above_Gunung_Bromo_-_Indonesia.jpg/330px-Startrails_above_Gunung_Bromo_-_Indonesia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nature","wikidata":"Q7860"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Michael_Maier_-_Atalanta_Fugiens_-_Mother_Earth.jpg/500px-Michael_Maier_-_Atalanta_Fugiens_-_Mother_Earth.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Michael_Maier_-_Atalanta_Fugiens_-_Mother_Earth.jpg/1280px-Michael_Maier_-_Atalanta_Fugiens_-_Mother_Earth.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Michael_Maier_-_Atalanta_Fugiens_-_Mother_Earth.jpg/500px-Michael_Maier_-_Atalanta_Fugiens_-_Mother_Earth.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mother Earth image, 17th century alchemical text, Atalanta Fugiens","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMichael_Maier_-_Atalanta_Fugiens_-_Mother_Earth.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/2023-07_SDCC_Jamie_Lee_Curtis_Mother_Nature.jpg/500px-2023-07_SDCC_Jamie_Lee_Curtis_Mother_Nature.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_ca